= Multi-agent system =

== Overview ==
A '''multi-agent system''' ('''MAS''') is a computational system composed of multiple interacting agents.

The agents can cooperate, compete, negotiate, coordinate, share information, pursue separate objectives, or combine these behaviors.

Multi-agent systems are an established area of artificial intelligence and predate large language models by decades.

== Agents and interaction ==
The agents in a multi-agent system can have:

* local observations;
* separate internal states;
* individual or shared goals;
* different capabilities;
* communication channels;
* incomplete knowledge of the global system.

System behavior emerges from both individual agent policies and their interactions.

== Coordination ==
Multi-agent problems can involve:

* task allocation;
* communication;
* distributed planning;
* coordination;
* negotiation;
* coalition formation;
* consensus;
* competition;
* mechanism design.

The appropriate model depends on whether agents share goals, have conflicting incentives, or operate under mixed motives.

== Multi-agent systems and distributed systems ==
A [[Distributed system]] contains computational components distributed across multiple machines or processes.

A multi-agent system specifically models multiple components as agents with action-selection or objective-directed behavior.

The concepts can overlap, but:

<pre>
multi-agent system ≠ distributed system
</pre>

== Multi-agent systems and distributed cognition ==
[[Distributed cognition]] studies cognition across people, artifacts, and environments.

A multi-agent system is a computational architecture/research field.

They should not be equated.

== LLM-based multi-agent systems ==
Modern systems sometimes use multiple LLM-based agents with different roles.

This is one application of MAS ideas, not the definition of the field.

A pipeline that merely calls the same model several times is not necessarily a multi-agent system unless the components function as interacting agents.

